1
00:00:01,140 --> 00:00:26,900
So today, first, let's take another look at conditional branch instructions. Conditional branch instructions are called Conditional Branch Instructions in English. They are mainly used in our code — as we know, besides sequential execution, we have things like the most basic conditional judgment in C, the if-else statement, which is actually carried out by these assembly instructions, these machine instructions.

2
00:00:26,900 --> 00:00:31,180
So in the conditional branch instructions of Risk5,

3
00:00:31,180 --> 00:00:33,160
there are several kinds,

4
00:00:33,160 --> 00:00:35,220
and let's take a look.

5
00:00:35,220 --> 00:00:38,180
There are BEQ, BNE, BLT,

6
00:00:38,180 --> 00:00:39,980
and these names

7
00:00:39,980 --> 00:00:41,020
actually have meanings.

8
00:00:41,020 --> 00:00:44,800
As for their specific meanings,

9
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:47,100
we can see them here, which I have also listed for you.

10
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:49,280
So for BEQ,

11
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:52,340
it is actually branch if equal.

12
00:00:52,340 --> 00:00:53,500
What that means is,

13
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:54,140
what does it mean?

14
00:00:54,140 --> 00:00:56,540
It checks whether two numbers are equal.

15
00:00:56,540 --> 00:00:57,920
If they are equal,

16
00:00:57,920 --> 00:00:59,320
then it jumps.

17
00:00:59,320 --> 00:01:01,280
Branch means to branch,

18
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:03,140
forking means jumping.

19
00:01:03,140 --> 00:01:06,220
The others are similar,

20
00:01:06,220 --> 00:01:08,400
BNE

21
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:10,980
Now, once you remember this English term,

22
00:01:10,980 --> 00:01:12,400
when you memorize this,

23
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:15,620
the name of the command becomes much easier to remember.

24
00:01:15,620 --> 00:01:18,280
This is one thing everyone should pay attention to.

25
00:01:18,280 --> 00:01:20,660
And there's another thing,

26
00:01:20,660 --> 00:01:24,160
which is these conditional branch instructions,

27
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:26,220
the encoding format they use,

28
00:01:26,220 --> 00:01:28,960
we call it B-type.

29
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:30,820
So why is it called B-type?

30
00:01:30,820 --> 00:01:32,200
Now you all understand what this B means, right?

31
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:33,540
It actually stands for branch,

32
00:01:33,540 --> 00:01:36,400
it comes from conditional branch instructions.

33
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:38,600
This encoding format,

34
00:01:38,600 --> 00:01:39,360
this encoding scheme,

35
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:40,160
you can take a look at it.

36
00:01:40,160 --> 00:01:41,960
There's a diagram down below,

37
00:01:41,960 --> 00:01:44,040
which I've laid out for you.

38
00:01:44,040 --> 00:01:45,260
As we can see from it,

39
00:01:45,260 --> 00:01:47,840
bits 0 to 6 are still,

40
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:49,060
that is, the lower bits 0 to 6,

41
00:01:49,060 --> 00:01:50,720
it still holds the opcode

42
00:01:50,720 --> 00:01:51,800
right

43
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:53,600
and then there's also

44
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:54,880
two fields that hold registers

45
00:01:54,880 --> 00:01:55,900
RS1 and RS2

46
00:01:55,900 --> 00:01:57,940
now as for function3

47
00:01:57,940 --> 00:01:59,560
it's used together with the opcode to

48
00:01:59,560 --> 00:02:01,300
uniquely identify the

49
00:02:01,300 --> 00:02:03,520
type of this instruction

50
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:05,380
and then there's also an immediate value

51
00:02:05,380 --> 00:02:06,060
as you can see

52
00:02:06,060 --> 00:02:07,360
this immediate value

53
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:09,420
now some of you might

54
00:02:09,420 --> 00:02:10,600
have some confusion about the

55
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:11,740
encoding method of this immediate

56
00:02:11,740 --> 00:02:12,960
and feel a bit puzzled

57
00:02:12,960 --> 00:02:14,260
which is that this immediate value

58
00:02:14,260 --> 00:02:15,760
is not kept together in one piece

59
00:02:15,760 --> 00:02:16,320
do you all see that

60
00:02:16,320 --> 00:02:18,320
that is, for the immediate value as a whole

61
00:02:18,320 --> 00:02:18,960
It is a

62
00:02:18,960 --> 00:02:21,940
The numbers written here are

63
00:02:21,940 --> 00:02:24,500
the lowest is bit 1

64
00:02:24,500 --> 00:02:25,760
and the highest goes up to

65
00:02:25,760 --> 00:02:28,000
bit 12

66
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:29,580
So you can see that

67
00:02:29,580 --> 00:02:33,040
for the full 12 bits of data

68
00:02:33,040 --> 00:02:34,160
they are not placed together

69
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:35,940
They are actually distributed across

70
00:02:35,940 --> 00:02:36,940
the entire

71
00:02:36,940 --> 00:02:40,140
encoding format, in two separate places

72
00:02:40,140 --> 00:02:41,780
And in the two places,

73
00:02:41,780 --> 00:02:43,240
the format is actually still

74
00:02:43,240 --> 00:02:45,380
bits 1 to 4 are here

75
00:02:45,380 --> 00:02:48,260
then it jumps over here and puts bits 5 to 10

76
00:02:48,260 --> 00:02:49,620
bits 5 to 10, then it jumps here again to

77
00:02:49,620 --> 00:02:51,900
put bit 11, and then bit 12 is placed here

78
00:02:51,900 --> 00:02:52,820
They are not placed consecutively

79
00:02:52,820 --> 00:02:53,420
Do you see that?

80
00:02:53,420 --> 00:02:56,140
Don't be surprised by this

81
00:02:56,140 --> 00:02:57,420
As you all know,

82
00:02:57,420 --> 00:02:59,680
this is a machine instruction.

83
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:01,340
The way machine instructions are encoded,

84
00:03:01,340 --> 00:03:02,760
it's really mainly considered

85
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:05,300
for the sake of the underlying hardware's

86
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:06,860
processing efficiency.

87
00:03:06,860 --> 00:03:08,380
So this encoding scheme

88
00:03:08,380 --> 00:03:10,480
is designed entirely for

89
00:03:10,480 --> 00:03:12,380
the processing efficiency of the underlying hardware.

90
00:03:12,380 --> 00:03:14,320
Now, when we're programming,

91
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:15,660
we don't actually need to worry about this.

92
00:03:15,660 --> 00:03:16,900
All we need to know is

93
00:03:16,900 --> 00:03:18,720
that these two parts

94
00:03:18,720 --> 00:03:21,200
together make up an immediate number --

95
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:23,200
a 12-bit immediate value --

96
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:24,620
and that's enough.

97
00:03:24,620 --> 00:03:26,740
That's one thing.

98
00:03:26,740 --> 00:03:28,880
And then there's another,

99
00:03:28,880 --> 00:03:29,760
which is this:

100
00:03:29,760 --> 00:03:30,580
we know that this

101
00:03:30,580 --> 00:03:31,760
So besides knowing that

102
00:03:31,760 --> 00:03:33,300
these conditional branch instructions,

103
00:03:33,300 --> 00:03:34,300
they operate on certain —

104
00:03:34,300 --> 00:03:35,980
that is, whether things are equal,

105
00:03:35,980 --> 00:03:36,660
not equal,

106
00:03:36,660 --> 00:03:37,880
they're divided into several groups like this.

107
00:03:37,880 --> 00:03:38,140
Right?

108
00:03:38,140 --> 00:03:38,760
As you can see,

109
00:03:38,760 --> 00:03:41,480
one checks for equality,

110
00:03:41,480 --> 00:03:43,340
and one branches if not equal.

111
00:03:43,340 --> 00:03:44,340
Then there's another one,

112
00:03:44,340 --> 00:03:46,500
which branches if less than.

113
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:48,000
And another one that branches if greater than or equal.

114
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:49,160
Less than and greater than or equal —

115
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:50,020
we know those are a pair,

116
00:03:50,020 --> 00:03:50,380
right?

117
00:03:50,380 --> 00:03:52,240
So that's one.

118
00:03:52,240 --> 00:03:54,060
And then there's another one, that is to say,

119
00:03:54,060 --> 00:03:55,260
there's also this one here —

120
00:03:55,260 --> 00:03:57,500
you see, apart from this BLT,

121
00:03:57,500 --> 00:03:58,520
There's also a BLTU.

122
00:03:58,520 --> 00:04:01,160
And for BGE, there's a BGEU.

123
00:04:01,160 --> 00:04:02,400
Now the only difference

124
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:03,560
is that it has an extra U at the end.

125
00:04:03,560 --> 00:04:05,500
So what does this U mean?

126
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:07,480
It stands for the unsigned concept.

127
00:04:07,480 --> 00:04:08,280
That is to say, when they

128
00:04:08,280 --> 00:04:09,820
If there's a U added,

129
00:04:09,820 --> 00:04:11,600
it means that when they compare,

130
00:04:11,600 --> 00:04:14,480
the comparison is done as unsigned numbers.

131
00:04:14,480 --> 00:04:15,520
In other words, the sign bit actually,

132
00:04:15,520 --> 00:04:17,520
you can just treat everything as a positive number.

133
00:04:17,520 --> 00:04:20,840
Now, if there's no U,

134
00:04:20,840 --> 00:04:22,720
it uses the signed representation.

135
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:24,980
So in this case, this immediate value's—

136
00:04:24,980 --> 00:04:26,780
I mean, this isn't an immediate value,

137
00:04:26,780 --> 00:04:28,600
it's the number stored in the register—

138
00:04:28,600 --> 00:04:31,080
if it's a positive or negative number,

139
00:04:31,080 --> 00:04:32,660
it does take part in the comparison.

140
00:04:32,660 --> 00:04:34,880
That is, the comparison is performed as signed values.

141
00:04:34,880 --> 00:04:37,640
This is when these instructions were designed.

142
00:04:37,640 --> 00:04:38,600
We need to pay attention to this.

143
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:42,560
So in total, the classification goes like this.

144
00:04:42,560 --> 00:04:45,080
It ends up being six in total.

145
00:04:45,080 --> 00:04:47,940
Six conditional branch instructions.

146
00:04:47,940 --> 00:04:52,760
You can actually memorize them in pairs.

147
00:04:52,760 --> 00:04:56,720
Now that we understand this,

148
00:04:56,720 --> 00:04:57,920
the last point is that

149
00:04:57,920 --> 00:04:59,700
because in the end it needs a jump,

150
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:01,060
when this jump happens,

151
00:05:01,060 --> 00:05:01,760
this target,

152
00:05:01,760 --> 00:05:03,300
the target address of the jump,

153
00:05:03,300 --> 00:05:04,340
how is it calculated?

154
00:05:04,340 --> 00:05:06,900
This is something we need to understand.

155
00:05:06,900 --> 00:05:09,000
As you can see,

156
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:11,700
the way it is calculated is right here.

157
00:05:11,700 --> 00:05:14,160
We've listed it out for you here,

158
00:05:14,160 --> 00:05:16,520
that is, the method for calculating the jump target address.

159
00:05:16,520 --> 00:05:18,160
And how is it calculated?

160
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:21,820
It is based on the immediate value in the encoding.

161
00:05:21,820 --> 00:05:23,520
Multiply this immediate by two.

162
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:25,540
After sign extension,

163
00:05:25,540 --> 00:05:26,620
pay attention here, read carefully.

164
00:05:26,620 --> 00:05:28,900
Of course, what I've done here is take the English from the specification

165
00:05:28,900 --> 00:05:30,520
and translate it into Chinese

166
00:05:30,520 --> 00:05:31,580
for everyone here.

167
00:05:31,580 --> 00:05:33,800
Once you've understood this,

168
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:36,280
go back and take a look at the standard itself.

169
00:05:36,280 --> 00:05:38,260
That might give you an even better understanding.

170
00:05:38,260 --> 00:05:41,200
So, first multiply this immediate by two,

171
00:05:41,200 --> 00:05:42,800
then after sign extension,

172
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:45,200
add it to the PC value,

173
00:05:45,200 --> 00:05:47,000
and only then can you get the final target address.

174
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:50,500
So what does this multiplying by 2 mean?

175
00:05:50,500 --> 00:05:51,520
Actually, what it means is

176
00:05:51,520 --> 00:05:53,560
it actually shows that

177
00:05:53,560 --> 00:05:55,040
the immediate it finally gets

178
00:05:55,040 --> 00:05:56,420
is definitely a multiple of 2.

179
00:05:56,420 --> 00:05:57,640
Do you all understand?

180
00:05:57,640 --> 00:05:59,140
It's definitely a multiple of 2.

181
00:05:59,140 --> 00:06:00,400
like 2,

182
00:06:00,400 --> 00:06:01,240
4,

183
00:06:01,240 --> 00:06:02,960
8, values like that.

184
00:06:02,960 --> 00:06:04,940
Now why is that the case?

185
00:06:04,940 --> 00:06:05,700
It's mainly because

186
00:06:05,700 --> 00:06:06,400
it's that

187
00:06:06,400 --> 00:06:07,840
as we mentioned earlier,

188
00:06:07,840 --> 00:06:08,740
in instruction encoding,

189
00:06:08,740 --> 00:06:10,160
when instructions are laid out in memory,

190
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:11,580
there's this concept of alignment.

191
00:06:11,580 --> 00:06:12,100
That is to say,

192
00:06:12,100 --> 00:06:13,600
each instruction

193
00:06:13,600 --> 00:06:16,020
may follow this... I mean,

194
00:06:16,020 --> 00:06:18,200
that is to say,

195
00:06:18,200 --> 00:06:20,260
of course, here in our case,

196
00:06:20,260 --> 00:06:21,440
since the exercises we'll do later

197
00:06:21,440 --> 00:06:23,860
don't involve these compressed instructions,

198
00:06:23,860 --> 00:06:25,580
but we know

199
00:06:25,580 --> 00:06:27,040
that, as we discussed earlier,

200
00:06:27,040 --> 00:06:28,440
this RISC-V...

201
00:06:28,440 --> 00:06:30,400
It supports this compressed instruction format.

202
00:06:30,400 --> 00:06:31,960
Now, as for compressed instructions,

203
00:06:31,960 --> 00:06:33,520
each instruction

204
00:06:33,520 --> 00:06:35,040
compared to our normal ones today,

205
00:06:35,040 --> 00:06:35,860
for normal instructions, we know

206
00:06:35,860 --> 00:06:37,320
how many bytes is one instruction?

207
00:06:37,320 --> 00:06:38,000
We covered this earlier,

208
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:38,480
you still remember, right?

209
00:06:38,480 --> 00:06:40,460
It's 4 bytes per instruction,

210
00:06:40,460 --> 00:06:40,800
right?

211
00:06:40,800 --> 00:06:42,640
But with the compressed format,

212
00:06:42,640 --> 00:06:44,500
it's an instruction extension,

213
00:06:44,500 --> 00:06:45,200
specifically the C extension.

214
00:06:45,200 --> 00:06:45,940
With the C extension,

215
00:06:45,940 --> 00:06:47,480
one instruction is

216
00:06:47,480 --> 00:06:48,640
half of that,

217
00:06:48,640 --> 00:06:51,340
and since it's half,

218
00:06:51,340 --> 00:06:53,060
it means a single instruction actually

219
00:06:53,060 --> 00:06:54,040
takes up 2 bytes,

220
00:06:54,040 --> 00:06:58,460
and multiplying that by 2,

221
00:06:58,460 --> 00:06:59,720
That's actually what this case is about.

222
00:06:59,720 --> 00:07:01,940
At least that's how I understand it.

223
00:07:01,940 --> 00:07:02,920
You can all look at it this way.

224
00:07:02,920 --> 00:07:03,540
Understand it like this.

225
00:07:03,540 --> 00:07:04,540
So, since

226
00:07:04,540 --> 00:07:06,000
this instruction

227
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:07,100
has a minimum alignment

228
00:07:07,100 --> 00:07:08,660
of two bytes,

229
00:07:08,660 --> 00:07:10,120
then when we

230
00:07:10,120 --> 00:07:11,900
encode it,

231
00:07:11,900 --> 00:07:13,100
that last bit behind it,

232
00:07:13,100 --> 00:07:14,980
that is, the one corresponding to two to the power of one,

233
00:07:14,980 --> 00:07:15,500
that bit,

234
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:16,620
can actually be omitted.

235
00:07:16,620 --> 00:07:17,440
We don't need to write it in.

236
00:07:17,440 --> 00:07:17,880
Think about it, right?

237
00:07:17,880 --> 00:07:18,760
Because it's always zero.

238
00:07:18,760 --> 00:07:20,280
Think about it — the lowest bit is always zero.

239
00:07:20,280 --> 00:07:21,600
In other words, its address,

240
00:07:21,600 --> 00:07:23,260
the last bit of the address is always zero.

241
00:07:23,260 --> 00:07:23,840
So this bit

242
00:07:23,840 --> 00:07:25,060
doesn't need to be part of the encoding.

243
00:07:25,060 --> 00:07:26,260
So this way, we can save one

244
00:07:26,260 --> 00:07:27,080
save one bit.

245
00:07:27,080 --> 00:07:28,320
You see what I mean, right?

246
00:07:28,320 --> 00:07:29,180
So when they

247
00:07:29,180 --> 00:07:30,860
designed the instructions,

248
00:07:30,860 --> 00:07:32,120
this is how they saved space.

249
00:07:32,120 --> 00:07:32,620
So

250
00:07:32,620 --> 00:07:33,300
if you look at it,

251
00:07:33,300 --> 00:07:33,960
if you look at this,

252
00:07:33,960 --> 00:07:35,080
this immediate,

253
00:07:35,080 --> 00:07:35,940
this number here,

254
00:07:35,940 --> 00:07:37,020
you can see it starts from 1,

255
00:07:37,020 --> 00:07:38,420
encoding begins at bit 1, right?

256
00:07:38,420 --> 00:07:38,820
So right here,

257
00:07:38,820 --> 00:07:39,560
there is no bit 0.

258
00:07:39,560 --> 00:07:40,240
Notice that, everyone.

259
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:40,980
No bit 0.

260
00:07:40,980 --> 00:07:43,500
So that means these 12 bits are actually the upper 12 bits.

261
00:07:43,500 --> 00:07:47,500
In fact, this whole address is really a 13-bit value.

262
00:07:47,500 --> 00:07:50,180
Now, with 13 bits, we also have to consider sign extension.

263
00:07:50,180 --> 00:07:52,320
That is, it can represent both positive and negative numbers.

264
00:07:52,320 --> 00:07:56,580
So its actual range is from negative 4096 to 4094.

265
00:07:56,580 --> 00:07:58,480
You can work this number out for yourselves when you get back.

266
00:07:58,480 --> 00:08:01,900
Based on the related concepts I just introduced to you,

267
00:08:01,900 --> 00:08:03,940
you can guess why it's this range.

268
00:08:03,940 --> 00:08:05,160
Why is it this range?

269
00:08:05,160 --> 00:08:07,580
Anyway,

270
00:08:07,580 --> 00:08:09,120
it eventually produces a value,

271
00:08:09,120 --> 00:08:11,960
and note that this value is added to the PC.

272
00:08:11,960 --> 00:08:16,740
So when it jumps, the jump is relative to the current PC value.

273
00:08:16,740 --> 00:08:18,060
Please keep that in mind too.

274
00:08:18,060 --> 00:08:20,300
In other words, based on the current PC value,

275
00:08:20,300 --> 00:08:24,740
it jumps to an address roughly 4K above or below,

276
00:08:24,740 --> 00:08:26,940
jumping to a new address to fetch the value.

277
00:08:26,940 --> 00:08:33,900
So that's how the target address is calculated.

278
00:08:33,900 --> 00:08:37,680
It's also an important concept for understanding jump and conditional branch instructions.

279
00:08:37,680 --> 00:08:41,620
Then, when it comes to the actual programming,

280
00:08:41,620 --> 00:08:43,240
when you're writing code,

281
00:08:43,240 --> 00:08:45,720
Actually, to make things easier for us programmers,

282
00:08:45,720 --> 00:08:49,380
we don't actually go and do it like this afterwards,

283
