We actually just need to take this label,

1134
00:34:49,040 --> 00:34:49,960
which is really just a

1135
00:34:49,960 --> 00:34:53,200
so-called label, which is just the name of a function,

1136
00:34:53,200 --> 00:34:54,920
and write it out and hand it over.

1137
00:34:54,920 --> 00:34:57,240
And once we hand it over,

1138
00:34:57,240 --> 00:34:59,240
the compiler and the linker

1139
00:34:59,240 --> 00:35:01,220
will compute the actual target address for us.

1140
00:35:01,220 --> 00:35:02,720
And then...

1141
00:35:02,720 --> 00:35:03,760
Take note of this

1142
00:35:03,760 --> 00:35:04,920
This JAL instruction

1143
00:35:04,920 --> 00:35:07,340
has an rd register

1144
00:35:07,340 --> 00:35:08,660
This rd register

1145
00:35:08,660 --> 00:35:09,240
as we just mentioned

1146
00:35:09,240 --> 00:35:10,120
what is it used to hold?

1147
00:35:10,120 --> 00:35:11,280
It holds

1148
00:35:11,280 --> 00:35:14,740
for this JAL instruction,

1149
00:35:14,740 --> 00:35:16,400
the address of the next instruction

1150
00:35:16,400 --> 00:35:17,200
is placed here

1151
00:35:17,200 --> 00:35:18,780
So that's why I marked this in red here

1152
00:35:18,780 --> 00:35:20,220
Basically, you can imagine it this way

1153
00:35:20,220 --> 00:35:20,900
Just imagine

1154
00:35:20,900 --> 00:35:23,260
that after we finish calling

1155
00:35:23,260 --> 00:35:24,400
the SUM function

1156
00:35:24,400 --> 00:35:26,240
there is definitely another instruction after it

1157
00:35:26,240 --> 00:35:27,700
and this instruction here

1158
00:35:27,700 --> 00:35:30,400
the address of this instruction

1159
00:35:30,400 --> 00:35:32,500
it will store it into

1160
00:35:32,500 --> 00:35:34,400
the X5 register

1161
00:35:34,400 --> 00:35:35,000
So everyone,

1162
00:35:35,000 --> 00:35:37,360
what is it stored here for?

1163
00:35:37,360 --> 00:35:38,880
What is it used for?

1164
00:35:38,880 --> 00:35:40,820
This brings us back to what we just talked about,

1165
00:35:40,820 --> 00:35:41,800
this return.

1166
00:35:41,800 --> 00:35:45,200
Now, this return statement,

1167
00:35:45,200 --> 00:35:46,640
it actually corresponds to,

1168
00:35:46,640 --> 00:35:47,420
that is, it is called,

1169
00:35:47,420 --> 00:35:49,000
we can translate it into,

1170
00:35:49,000 --> 00:35:50,380
the JALR instruction.

1171
00:35:50,380 --> 00:35:52,060
As for the JALR instruction,

1172
00:35:52,060 --> 00:35:52,840
as we mentioned earlier,

1173
00:35:52,840 --> 00:35:54,180
what is its purpose?

1174
00:35:54,180 --> 00:35:55,540
Based on the way it's written here,

1175
00:35:55,540 --> 00:35:56,360
let's understand,

1176
00:35:56,360 --> 00:35:57,620
in practice,

1177
00:35:57,620 --> 00:36:00,740
how does it compute the target address?

1178
00:36:00,740 --> 00:36:02,900
It uses 0x5.

1179
00:36:02,900 --> 00:36:03,720
What does 0x5 mean?

1180
00:36:03,720 --> 00:36:06,760
It means it's based on the value in x5.

1181
00:36:06,760 --> 00:36:08,600
add an immediate value.

1182
00:36:08,600 --> 00:36:10,080
This immediate value is simply 0.

1183
00:36:10,080 --> 00:36:11,360
So, in that case,

1184
00:36:11,360 --> 00:36:13,260
the target address it jumps to,

1185
00:36:13,260 --> 00:36:14,440
is actually equal to x5.

1186
00:36:14,440 --> 00:36:15,040
Think about it, isn't that right?

1187
00:36:15,040 --> 00:36:17,360
Because the way it computes the target address,

1188
00:36:17,360 --> 00:36:19,140
is x5 plus 0,

1189
00:36:19,140 --> 00:36:21,540
and that's how it gets it.

1190
00:36:21,540 --> 00:36:23,260
Now, as you all know, x5,

1191
00:36:23,260 --> 00:36:25,560
this statement here is executed first.

1192
00:36:25,560 --> 00:36:26,320
When it executes,

1193
00:36:26,320 --> 00:36:27,700
it puts a value into x5.

1194
00:36:27,700 --> 00:36:28,480
What value does it put in?

1195
00:36:28,480 --> 00:36:30,900
It puts the address

1196
00:36:30,900 --> 00:36:32,620
of the next instruction over here.

1197
00:36:32,620 --> 00:36:33,120
Think about it, isn't that right?

1198
00:36:33,120 --> 00:36:35,940
So when you add 0 here,

1199
00:36:35,940 --> 00:36:37,260
the address it actually jumps to,

1200
00:36:37,260 --> 00:36:39,060
doesn't it end up jumping back over here again?

1201
00:36:39,060 --> 00:36:39,780
Think about it, everyone.

1202
00:36:39,780 --> 00:36:41,480
Is that what it means?

1203
00:36:41,480 --> 00:36:43,080
Can you figure it out?

1204
00:36:43,080 --> 00:36:44,800
Everyone needs to get this straight.

1205
00:36:44,800 --> 00:36:47,140
With these two instructions, there's basically no problem.

1206
00:36:47,140 --> 00:36:49,240
That is, we use this register,

1207
00:36:49,240 --> 00:36:50,280
the register x5,

1208
00:36:50,280 --> 00:36:53,280
and when it jumps over here,

1209
00:36:53,280 --> 00:36:55,140
it actually does one more thing,

1210
00:36:55,140 --> 00:36:57,400
namely, it takes the address of its next instruction

1211
00:36:57,400 --> 00:36:59,160
and saves it into x5.

1212
00:36:59,160 --> 00:37:00,160
It stores it there.

1213
00:37:00,160 --> 00:37:01,380
What is it stored there for?

1214
00:37:01,380 --> 00:37:03,020
It's for the return.

1215
00:37:03,020 --> 00:37:03,900
You see what I mean,

1216
00:37:03,900 --> 00:37:04,940
it's for its use.

1217
00:37:04,940 --> 00:37:05,800
When it uses it,

1218
00:37:05,800 --> 00:37:06,740
it uses jr,

1219
00:37:06,740 --> 00:37:08,700
and based on the value of x5,

1220
00:37:08,700 --> 00:37:11,360
it computes the return address.

1221
00:37:11,360 --> 00:37:12,300
And that's the return address.

1222
00:37:12,300 --> 00:37:13,260
So it jumps back again.

1223
00:37:13,260 --> 00:37:14,780
So the whole process is this:

1224
00:37:14,780 --> 00:37:16,840
jump up and execute,

1225
00:37:16,840 --> 00:37:18,660
then return and jump back.

1226
00:37:18,660 --> 00:37:20,480
It makes sense, right?

1227
00:37:20,480 --> 00:37:21,580
This is how we implement

1228
00:37:21,580 --> 00:37:23,500
the process of a function call and return.

1229
00:37:23,500 --> 00:37:26,020
You can see that, right?

1230
00:37:26,020 --> 00:37:28,740
So everyone, take this code

1231
00:37:28,740 --> 00:37:30,180
and combine it with what we covered last time,

1232
00:37:30,180 --> 00:37:31,140
and go over it yourselves.

1233
00:37:31,140 --> 00:37:32,020
I mean the code,

1234
00:37:32,020 --> 00:37:33,520
take a look at it yourself when you get back.

1235
00:37:33,520 --> 00:37:37,140
Just go through the code in the course material,

1236
00:37:37,140 --> 00:37:39,020
and make sure you understand the concept I just described.

1237
00:37:39,020 --> 00:37:51,720
As for the debugging process,

1238
00:37:51,720 --> 00:37:55,160
I won't walk you through it,

1239
00:37:55,160 --> 00:38:03,720
since we already spent a bit of time earlier.

1240
00:38:03,720 --> 00:38:06,440
Let's finish covering these jump instructions.

1241
00:38:06,440 --> 00:38:07,080
Let's call it a day.

1242
00:38:07,080 --> 00:38:11,000
Okay, this code example,

1243
00:38:11,000 --> 00:38:12,040
we'll just go over it on our own.

1244
00:38:12,040 --> 00:38:13,380
I won't demonstrate it for you again.

1245
00:38:13,380 --> 00:38:16,120
So basically,

1246
00:38:16,120 --> 00:38:18,060
for unconditional jumps

1247
00:38:18,060 --> 00:38:18,740
we have two instructions.

1248
00:38:18,740 --> 00:38:19,980
One is j,

1249
00:38:19,980 --> 00:38:21,420
and the other is jal, or jump and link register.

1250
00:38:21,420 --> 00:38:22,060
Two instructions.

1251
00:38:22,060 --> 00:38:23,160
Saying the full names,

1252
00:38:23,160 --> 00:38:24,220
seems to flow a bit better.

1253
00:38:24,220 --> 00:38:26,120
The abbreviations are a bit of a tongue-twister.

1254
00:38:26,120 --> 00:38:28,320
Now, here,

1255
00:38:28,320 --> 00:38:29,040
let's bring up one more concept.

1256
00:38:29,040 --> 00:38:30,100
That is, as we can see,

1257
00:38:30,100 --> 00:38:31,820
just now when we introduced

1258
00:38:31,820 --> 00:38:33,700
the jal instruction to you,

1259
00:38:33,700 --> 00:38:34,540
as we mentioned earlier,

1260
00:38:34,540 --> 00:38:36,000
as for its jump range,

1261
00:38:36,000 --> 00:38:37,660
It's based on the PC

1262
00:38:37,660 --> 00:38:38,400
up and down

1263
00:38:38,400 --> 00:38:39,500
that is,

1264
00:38:39,500 --> 00:38:41,260
that's already a very large range

1265
00:38:41,260 --> 00:38:43,160
meaning it can jump within a 1-megabyte range

1266
00:38:43,160 --> 00:38:43,420
right?

1267
00:38:43,420 --> 00:38:45,080
But as we know

1268
00:38:45,080 --> 00:38:46,660
the programs we're writing now

1269
00:38:46,660 --> 00:38:49,300
might exceed 1 megabyte in size

1270
00:38:49,300 --> 00:38:51,180
The programs you're writing now

1271
00:38:51,180 --> 00:38:53,000
if the program you write is fairly small

1272
00:38:53,000 --> 00:38:53,660
it might compile down to

1273
00:38:53,660 --> 00:38:55,480
roughly a few K or a few tens of K

1274
00:38:55,480 --> 00:38:57,000
which means its range

1275
00:38:57,000 --> 00:38:57,720
is about that size

1276
00:38:57,720 --> 00:38:57,940
right?

1277
00:38:57,940 --> 00:39:00,480
But as your program grows

1278
00:39:00,480 --> 00:39:01,360
just imagine

1279
00:39:01,360 --> 00:39:03,680
when you run some large programs nowadays

1280
00:39:03,680 --> 00:39:05,980
a single executable can get really big

1281
00:39:05,980 --> 00:39:06,940
It might exceed 1 megabyte.

1282
00:39:06,940 --> 00:39:08,020
So at that point,

1283
00:39:08,020 --> 00:39:10,880
if you try to jump within a range that exceeds 1 megabyte,

1284
00:39:10,880 --> 00:39:13,980
then simply using an instruction like JAL,

1285
00:39:13,980 --> 00:39:15,820
well, that's actually going to cause problems.

1286
00:39:15,820 --> 00:39:17,460
Think about it — wouldn't that be an issue?

1287
00:39:17,460 --> 00:39:19,240
So what do we do?

1288
00:39:19,240 --> 00:39:21,600
The people who designed the assembly instructions

1289
00:39:21,600 --> 00:39:23,460
have definitely thought this through for us.

1290
00:39:23,460 --> 00:39:26,380
That is to say, how to handle longer-distance jumps.

1291
00:39:26,380 --> 00:39:30,100
In terms of data, we generally call this a 'far jump'.

1292
00:39:30,100 --> 00:39:33,540
So when you look at materials later on,

1293
00:39:33,540 --> 00:39:34,160
you'll often see

1294
00:39:34,160 --> 00:39:38,860
that relative to this 'far jump',

1295
00:39:38,860 --> 00:39:40,220
there's what we call a 'near jump',

1296
00:39:40,220 --> 00:39:42,240
which is a short-distance jump.

1297
00:39:42,240 --> 00:39:45,460
Because it can only jump up or down about 1 megabyte.

1298
00:39:45,460 --> 00:39:47,720
But if you need to jump beyond that range,

1299
00:39:47,720 --> 00:39:50,780
that's what's called a long-distance jump.

1300
00:39:50,780 --> 00:39:53,100
So how do we implement a long-distance jump?

1301
00:39:53,100 --> 00:39:54,680
Let's actually think about it this way.

1302
00:39:54,680 --> 00:39:55,760
We actually talked about this earlier.

1303
00:39:55,760 --> 00:39:57,020
When we construct a large number,

1304
00:39:57,020 --> 00:39:58,160
remember what method we used?

1305
00:39:58,160 --> 00:40:01,720
We introduced this concept called AUIPC, right?

1306
00:40:01,720 --> 00:40:02,720
Remember that?

1307
00:40:02,720 --> 00:40:04,740
By the same logic,

1308
00:40:04,740 --> 00:40:05,740
we can also,

1309
00:40:05,740 --> 00:40:09,600
basically bring in the AUIPC instruction,

1310
00:40:09,600 --> 00:40:11,380
to help us construct a large number,

1311
00:40:11,380 --> 00:40:13,460
and implement this long-distance jump.

1312
00:40:13,460 --> 00:40:14,600
On this,

1313
00:40:14,600 --> 00:40:16,140
let me just briefly mention it.

1314
00:40:16,140 --> 00:40:18,480
I won't go into the details here.

1315
00:40:18,480 --> 00:40:19,640
Just be aware that

1316
00:40:19,640 --> 00:40:20,820
there's such a concept, and that's enough.

1317
00:40:20,820 --> 00:40:23,780
Okay.

1318
00:40:23,780 --> 00:40:26,320
So that's the long-distance jump I just talked about.

1319
00:40:26,320 --> 00:40:28,700
Now there's another concept,

1320
00:40:28,700 --> 00:40:30,500
which is that when we do jumps,

1321
00:40:30,500 --> 00:40:31,800
Actually, there's no need to return.

1322
00:40:31,800 --> 00:40:33,400
So when there's no need to return,

1323
00:40:33,400 --> 00:40:35,280
let's connect this with the example we just discussed.

1324
00:40:35,280 --> 00:40:36,600
When we returned just now,

1325
00:40:36,600 --> 00:40:37,320
take a look at this example.

1326
00:40:37,320 --> 00:40:39,640
What did we actually have to do?

1327
00:40:39,640 --> 00:40:41,480
We had to take the return address,

1328
00:40:41,480 --> 00:40:43,400
and save it in a register,

1329
00:40:43,400 --> 00:40:43,700
right?

1330
00:40:43,700 --> 00:40:46,200
And then use it when returning.

1331
00:40:46,200 --> 00:40:47,300
So now, since we're...

1332
00:40:47,300 --> 00:40:49,400
Suppose I call a function now,

1333
00:40:49,400 --> 00:40:50,580
and this function doesn't need to return.

1334
00:40:50,580 --> 00:40:52,260
That is, the program simply ends.

1335
00:40:52,260 --> 00:40:53,480
Take this program for example,

1336
00:40:53,480 --> 00:40:54,240
this is just an exit.

1337
00:40:54,240 --> 00:40:55,860
Once it exits, it's done.

1338
00:40:55,860 --> 00:40:56,820
It doesn't execute,

1339
00:40:56,820 --> 00:40:57,880
it doesn't continue executing.

1340
00:40:57,880 --> 00:40:59,680
So what do we do in this case?

1341
00:40:59,680 --> 00:41:00,540
So actually it's very simple.

1342
00:41:00,540 --> 00:41:02,400
We just don't use a register to

1343
00:41:02,400 --> 00:41:03,940
save this return address, and that's it.

1344
00:41:03,940 --> 00:41:04,560
Think about it, isn't that right?

1345
00:41:04,560 --> 00:41:08,060
But according to the requirement of this instruction,

1346
00:41:08,060 --> 00:41:09,620
here we must fill in a

1347
00:41:09,620 --> 00:41:11,300
register name.

1348
00:41:11,300 --> 00:41:13,400
So which register should we use?

1349
00:41:13,400 --> 00:41:15,440
This brings us to what we talked about earlier.

1350
00:41:15,440 --> 00:41:17,600
When we designed RISC-V,

1351
00:41:17,600 --> 00:41:18,900
it has a special register.

1352
00:41:18,900 --> 00:41:19,660
You still remember, right?

1353
00:41:19,660 --> 00:41:20,620
It's called x0.

1354
00:41:20,620 --> 00:41:24,120
This register is actually very special,

1355
00:41:24,120 --> 00:41:24,560
right?

1356
00:41:24,560 --> 00:41:27,460
Reading this register always gives you 0,

1357
00:41:27,460 --> 00:41:30,400
and writing data into this register is completely meaningless.

1358
00:41:30,400 --> 00:41:33,900
This actually means this register cannot be used to hold any value for us.

1359
00:41:33,900 --> 00:41:38,760
So if we want to implement this kind of non-returning jump,

1360
00:41:38,760 --> 00:41:43,600
actually, as you can see here, they all use x0.

1361
00:41:43,600 --> 00:41:46,480
So to make things easier for everyone to write,

1362
00:41:46,480 --> 00:41:49,940
this way of writing is replaced with some pseudo-instructions.

1363
00:41:49,940 --> 00:41:52,760
That is to say, as long as it's a J or a JR,

1364
00:41:52,760 --> 00:41:56,080
then what actually happens is that the rd field is

1365
00:41:56,080 --> 00:41:58,100
replaced with x0.

1366
00:41:58,100 --> 00:42:00,240
That achieves this kind of jump that doesn't return.

1367
00:42:00,240 --> 00:42:01,160
So this kind of thing is

1368
00:42:01,160 --> 00:42:05,740
for when you don't need to return,

1369
00:42:05,740 --> 00:42:07,140
you can use this kind of instruction to implement it.

1370
00:42:07,140 --> 00:42:10,820
Just something for everyone to be aware of.

1371
00:42:10,820 --> 00:42:18,480
Alright, having said that,

1372
00:42:18,480 --> 00:42:24,480
we've basically covered conditional jump instructions as well.

1373
00:42:24,480 --> 00:42:25,060
So finally,

1374
00:42:25,060 --> 00:42:26,720
let me give everyone a brief summary.

1375
00:42:26,720 --> 00:42:28,380
Up to this point,

1376
00:42:28,380 --> 00:42:30,620
of all the things we've covered,

1377
00:42:30,620 --> 00:42:31,660
one concept is

1378
00:42:31,660 --> 00:42:32,820
the concept of the addressed mode.

1379
00:42:32,820 --> 00:42:34,940
So the so-called addressed mode

1380
00:42:34,940 --> 00:42:35,640
means that

1381
00:42:35,640 --> 00:42:36,820
In an instruction,

1382
00:42:36,820 --> 00:42:38,500
one way to locate an operand

1383
00:42:38,500 --> 00:42:39,840
or its address

1384
00:42:39,840 --> 00:42:41,400
Now, from everything we've covered so far,

1385
00:42:41,400 --> 00:42:42,400
across all the instructions,

1386
00:42:42,400 --> 00:42:43,640
the addressing modes involved

1387
00:42:43,640 --> 00:42:44,940
— I'll briefly summarize them here for you.

1388
00:42:44,940 --> 00:42:46,640
let me give you a quick summary here.

1389
00:42:46,640 --> 00:42:48,460
The first one is called

1390
00:42:48,460 --> 00:42:49,460
immediate addressing.

1391
00:42:49,460 --> 00:42:50,820
It's very simple:

1392
00:42:50,820 --> 00:42:51,960
the operand

1393
00:42:51,960 --> 00:42:52,860
is the instruction itself.

1394
00:42:52,860 --> 00:42:54,520
Here's an example,

1395
00:42:54,520 --> 00:42:55,160
you probably remember it,

1396
00:42:55,160 --> 00:42:55,840
addi,

1397
00:42:55,840 --> 00:42:57,780
which is of course an addition instruction.

1398
00:42:57,780 --> 00:42:58,580
With this instruction,

1399
00:42:58,580 --> 00:42:59,980
the number it operates on

1400
00:42:59,980 --> 00:43:01,460
is actually given by the instruction itself

1401
00:43:01,460 --> 00:43:02,540
embedded in this instruction

1402
00:43:02,540 --> 00:43:02,960
isn't it?

1403
00:43:02,960 --> 00:43:04,420
the number 20 here

1404
00:43:04,420 --> 00:43:05,920
isn't it embedded in this instruction?

1405
00:43:05,920 --> 00:43:07,140
its operand

1406
00:43:07,140 --> 00:43:08,800
is directly embedded in this instruction

1407
00:43:08,800 --> 00:43:09,800
So we call this

1408
00:43:09,800 --> 00:43:10,960
an immediate operand

1409
00:43:10,960 --> 00:43:11,840
It doesn't need to

1410
00:43:11,840 --> 00:43:13,280
it's not stored in some special place

1411
00:43:13,280 --> 00:43:14,540
it's right there in the instruction

1412
00:43:14,540 --> 00:43:15,820
This is called an immediate operand

1413
00:43:15,820 --> 00:43:17,640
Now, there's another type

1414
00:43:17,640 --> 00:43:20,500
which we call a register operand

1415
00:43:20,500 --> 00:43:21,900
With a register operand,

1416
00:43:21,900 --> 00:43:23,540
the data it operates on

1417
00:43:23,540 --> 00:43:25,180
is stored in a register

1418
00:43:25,180 --> 00:43:28,640
and we access the register to get the operand

1419
00:43:28,640 --> 00:43:31,240
A typical example is the add instruction

1420
00:43:31,240 --> 00:43:31,840
right?

1421
00:43:31,840 --> 00:43:36,000
In this add instruction, all the operands

1422
00:43:36,000 --> 00:43:37,420
are kept in registers,

1423
00:43:37,420 --> 00:43:38,580
x6 and x7.

1424
00:43:38,580 --> 00:43:39,520
That's one type.

1425
00:43:39,520 --> 00:43:42,800
Now the third type is called base-plus-offset addressing.

1426
00:43:42,800 --> 00:43:44,380
Base-plus-offset addressing means

1427
00:43:44,380 --> 00:43:47,020
it works by using what?

1428
00:43:47,020 --> 00:43:49,180
By using a base plus an offset.

1429
00:43:49,180 --> 00:43:50,480
So the so-called base,

1430
00:43:50,480 --> 00:43:53,140
its base address is kept in a register.

1431
00:43:53,140 --> 00:43:54,160
Then,

1432
00:43:54,160 --> 00:43:56,020
inside the instruction itself,

1433
00:43:56,020 --> 00:43:57,480
you place an immediate value,

1434
00:43:57,480 --> 00:43:58,580
place an immediate value,

1435
00:43:58,580 --> 00:43:59,460
and then

1436
00:43:59,460 --> 00:44:02,000
add that immediate value to the value in the register,

1437
00:44:02,000 --> 00:44:03,360
which gives you an address.

1438
00:44:03,360 --> 00:44:04,400
Then, based on that address,

1439
00:44:04,400 --> 00:44:07,100
you go and find the operand you want to operate on.

1440
00:44:07,100 --> 00:44:08,780
That's the third type.

1441
00:44:08,780 --> 00:44:09,940
This is called register-relative addressing.

1442
00:44:09,940 --> 00:44:11,400
And then the last kind,

1443
00:44:11,400 --> 00:44:12,540
which is what we

1444
00:44:12,540 --> 00:44:14,840
have already seen several times earlier,

1445
00:44:14,840 --> 00:44:17,260
is that we take a PC value,

1446
00:44:17,260 --> 00:44:19,340
base it on the PC value,

1447
00:44:19,340 --> 00:44:21,600
and add an offset to it,

1448
00:44:21,600 --> 00:44:24,180
to get the final address.

1449
00:44:24,180 --> 00:44:26,340
This is called PC-relative addressing.

1450
00:44:26,340 --> 00:44:27,620
So here,

1451
00:44:27,620 --> 00:44:29,020
regarding this,

1452
00:44:29,020 --> 00:44:30,280
the concept of addressing modes,

1453
00:44:30,280 --> 00:44:31,360
it is really also

1454
00:44:31,360 --> 00:44:32,540
an important concept in

1455
00:44:32,540 --> 00:44:33,460
our assembly instructions.

1456
00:44:33,460 --> 00:44:34,620
So here,

1457
00:44:34,620 --> 00:44:35,840
let's take this opportunity

1458
00:44:35,840 --> 00:44:37,140
to summarize this for everyone here,

1459
00:44:37,140 --> 00:44:38,540
so you can get familiar with

1460
00:44:38,540 --> 00:44:40,100
this related knowledge.

1461
00:44:40,100 --> 00:44:43,470
So, this part about

1462
00:44:43,470 --> 00:44:44,850
the assembly instructions

1463
00:44:44,850 --> 00:44:47,050
is basically where we'll leave it for now.

1464
00:44:47,050 --> 00:44:48,590
Let's take a short break.
